From 66d41e08279ac51b3a1a56a37553329774f40f2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Struan Bartlett <struan.bartlett@NewsNow.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 02:02:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] passthrough: Add --submount-cache and --cache-path

Let one virtiofsd apply different cache policies to different mounts
within the shared directory, e.g. --cache=always for an image's root
filesystem and auto for volumes and bind mounts that are changed from
outside the guest.

A mount takes the policy of the longest --cache-path that is its mount
point or a path-segment prefix of it; else --submount-cache; else
--cache.  Configured paths are normalised lexically (bytes, not
necessarily UTF-8) and need not exist or be mount points: mounts that
appear later are matched when a lookup first crosses into them, using
their mount point from the sandbox's second mountinfo description.  An
inode on the same mount as its parent directory takes the parent's
policy.  Unique mount IDs (Linux 6.8+) let us remember each mount's
policy rather than read mountinfo again for each new inode at its root;
if a mount point cannot be found, the most conservative configured
policy applies.

A known inode keeps the policy it was created with: every live inode
pins its mount, so a mount ID can only be reused once all of its inodes
are gone.

Without the new options, behaviour is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
